/**
 * @module log
 *
 * Logging for Zuri applications and modules. Logs are sent to one or
 * more transports, such as the console, a file or a transport of your
 * own.
 *
 * The defaults suit most programs, so you can start logging without any
 * configuration:
 *
 * ```zuri-repl
 * %> import log
 * %> log.info('Starting my application...')
 * '2025-03-07T08:00:33+01:00 INFO [.]: Starting my application...'
 * ```
 *
 * > **Note**
 * >
 * > The `[.]` is there because this example runs in the REPL.
 * > By default, each log message includes the name of the
 * > application it came from, so that several applications can
 * > log to the same place without confusion.
 * >
 * > To change the name, set it on the transport with
 * > [[log.Transport.set_name]].
 *
 * Everything is configured at the module level, so the configuration
 * applies to every file and module for the lifetime of the application.
 *
 * The module includes transports for logging to the console
 * ([[log.ConsoleTransport]]) and to files ([[log.FileTransport]]),
 * which cover the needs of most applications, and you can also write
 * your own.
 *
 * The default transport, returned by [[log.default_transport()]], is a
 * [[log.ConsoleTransport]], and it's enabled unless you've disabled it.
 * This example adds a file transport, to log to a file on disk as well:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import log
 *
 * var transport = log.FileTransport('mylog.log')
 * log.add_transport(transport)
 *
 * log.info('Finished setting up file log...')
 * ```
 *
 * The file `mylog.log` now contains something like this:
 *
 * ```sh
 * 2025-03-01T12:00:00+01:00 INFO [tmp]: Finished setting up file log...
 * ```
 *
 * The message still appears on the console as well. To log only to the
 * file, there are two ways to turn off the console output.
 *
 * The first is to disable the default transport:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * log.default_transport().disable()
 * ```
 *
 * The transport stays registered, so you can enable it again at any
 * time:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * log.default_transport().enable()
 * ```
 *
 * Every transport has `enable()` and `disable()`, because they're
 * inherited from [[log.Transport]].
 *
 * The second way is to remove the transport from the list of registered
 * transports:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * log.remove_transport(log.default_transport())
 * ```
 *
 * In that case, you have to register the transport again if you want to
 * log to the console later. The same applies to every kind of
 * transport.
 *
 * To create a transport of your own, write a class that extends
 * [[log.Transport]] and implements at least the `write()` method.
 *
 * This example creates a transport that writes structured JSON to the
 * console:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * # my_custom_transport.zu
 *
 * import log { Transport }
 * import json
 * import enum
 *
 * class JsonConsoleTransport < Transport {
 *   format(records, level, context) {
 *     return {
 *       records,
 *       level,
 *     }
 *   }
 *
 *   write(message, level) {
 *     echo json.encode({
 *       logtime: time(),
 *       records: message.records,
 *       name: self.get_name(),
 *       level: enum.to_value_dict(log.LogLevel)[message.level]
 *     })
 *   }
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import log
 * import .my_custom_transport { JsonConsoleTransport }
 *
 * log.add_transport(JsonConsoleTransport())
 *
 * log.info('Finished setting up json console log transport...')
 * ```
 *
 * Running this prints something like:
 *
 * ```sh
 * {"logtime":1741376459,"records":["Finished setting up json console log transport..."],"name":"tmp","level":"Info"}
 * ```
 *
 * You can have several transports at once, each logging from a
 * different level. [[log.Transport.set_level]], which every transport
 * inherits, sets the lowest level that a transport logs.
 *
 * The [[log.set_level]] function sets the lowest level for all
 * transports:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import log
 *
 * log.set_level(log.Warning)
 *
 * log.info('Finished setting up json console log transport...')
 * ```
 *
 * This doesn't print anything, because [[log.Info]] is lower than
 * [[log.Warning]].
 *
 * Every `Transport` has a `format()` method, but you can replace it by
 * passing a function to [[log.Transport.set_formatter]]. That lets you
 * use the same formatter for several transports.
 *
 * In the previous example, if you wanted every transport to write JSON,
 * it would be better to write a formatting function and use it for each
 * transport, instead of writing a `JsonConsoleTransport`:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import log
 * import json
 * import date
 *
 * def json_format(records, level, transport) {
 *   return json.encode({
 *     time: date().format(transport.get_time_format()),
 *     level: log.get_level_name(level),
 *     name: transport.get_name(),
 *     records,
 *   })
 * }
 *
 * var file_transport = log.FileTransport('mylog.log')
 * log.add_transport(file_transport)
 *
 * log.default_transport().set_formatter(json_format)
 * file_transport.set_formatter(json_format)
 *
 * log.debug('This is a debug information')
 * ```
 *
 * > **Note**
 * >
 * > The `log` module can be called as a function. If you just
 * > want to log something quickly and don't care about levels,
 * > call the module itself, which logs at level [[log.None]]:
 * >
 * > ```zuri
 * > import log
 * >
 * > log('An anonymous log!')
 * > ```
 * >
 * > This skips every level filter, including the global
 * > [[log.set_level]] threshold, on every enabled transport.
 * > Only [[log.Transport.disable]] can stop it.
 *
 * ## Structured and named logging
 *
 * If the last argument of a log call is a dictionary, it's treated as
 * structured fields, not as part of the message, and written as
 * `key=value` pairs after the message:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * log.info('user signed in', { user_id: 42 })
 * # ... INFO [.]: user signed in user_id=42
 * ```
 *
 * For a subsystem that should label all of its log messages with a
 * name, and optionally a fixed set of fields, use [[log.get_logger]]
 * instead of the module-level functions:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * var db_log = log.get_logger('db').bind({ pool: 'primary' })
 * db_log.warn('slow query', { duration_ms: 850 })
 * # ... WARNING [db]: slow query pool=primary duration_ms=850
 * ```
 *
 * @copyright 2025, Richard Ore and The Zuri Contributors
 */

import @.level { * }
import @.transport { * }
import @.console { * }
import @.file { * }
import @.dispatch { * }
import @.logger { * }
